==English==
{{was wotd|2018|June|16}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|winsom|winsǒm}}, {{m|enm|winsome}}, {{m|enm|winsum}}, {{m|enm|wunsum||fair, pleasing to the senses; agreeable, gracious, pleasant; generous; of situations: favourable, propitious}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wynsum||joyful, merry, pleasant; winsome}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=winsǒm, adj.|id=MED52922|accessdate=19 March 2018}}</ref> from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wunisamaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*wunjōsamaz||joyful}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*wunjō||delight, desire, joy}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wenh₁-||to love; to wish}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|winne|t1=(''obsolete'') delight, joy, pleasure|some}}. The word is cognate with {{cog|gmh|wunnesam||delightful, joyful; winsome}} (modern {{cog|de|Wonne||bliss, delight, joy}}), {{cog|ang|wynn||delight, joy, pleasure, rapture}}, {{cog|sco|winsome}}, {{m|sco|wunsome||charming, comely, pleasing}}. ''See also'' {{m|en|winne}}, {{m|en|winly}}.

